## Typed client

Every method has four generics: `get<T = any, R = AxiosResponseDefault, D = any, P = any>` where `T` is the response body, `R` overrides the return type, `D` is the request body, and `P` is the query-params type (`params?: P` in `AxiosRequestConfig`). `axios.create()` returns `AxiosInstance` and, in this version, takes no type parameters itself, so you type at call sites or behind wrapper functions:

```ts
import axios from "axios";
import type { AxiosInstance, AxiosResponse, InternalAxiosRequestConfig } from "axios";

export const api: AxiosInstance = axios.create({
  baseURL: "https://api.example.com",
  timeout: 5000,
});

export interface Post { userId: number; id: number; title: string; }
export interface ApiErrorBody { message: string; code: number; }

// explicit return type gives maximum safety
export const getPost = (id: number): Promise<Post> =>
  api.get<Post>(`/posts/${id}`).then((res: AxiosResponse<Post>) => res.data);
```

For request interceptors use `InternalAxiosRequestConfig` (not `AxiosRequestConfig`) in v1.x, since at runtime the config has been merged with defaults.

## Narrowing `AxiosError` in catch blocks

`catch` values are `unknown` under `useUnknownInCatchVariables`, and `AxiosError<T = unknown>` keeps `response` optional — so the guard proves "this is an AxiosError," not "a response exists." The documented and safe narrowing is the `isAxiosError` type guard (`payload is AxiosError<T, D, P>`), which at runtime checks `isObject(payload) && payload.isAxiosError === true` — a flag check, deliberately not `instanceof`, so it also works when the caller bundles a different axios copy than your app:

```ts
import axios, { AxiosError } from "axios";
import type { ApiErrorBody } from "./api";

try {
  await api.get("/protected");
} catch (error) {
  if (axios.isAxiosError<ApiErrorBody>(error)) {
    if (error.response) {
      // server answered non-2xx: data is typed as ApiErrorBody
      console.error(error.response.status, error.response.data.message);
    } else if (error.request) {
      // request was sent, no response came back
      console.error(error.code); // e.g. AxiosError.ERR_NETWORK, ECONNABORTED
    } else {
      // setup failed before the request was sent
      console.error(error.message, error.config);
    }
    return;
  }
  // not an axios error; rethrow or handle as a plain unknown
  throw error;
}
```

Key details from the type declaration:

- Pass `T` explicitly to `isAxiosError<T>`: the class defaults `T` to `unknown` but the guard defaults it to `any`, so `axios.isAxiosError(error)` silently types `error.response.data` as `any` unless you specify it.
- `error.response?`, `error.config?`, `error.code?`, `error.request?`, and `error.status?` are all optional even after narrowing — always branch on `error.response` first.
- Compare `error.code` against the static constants on `AxiosError` (`ERR_BAD_REQUEST`, `ERR_BAD_RESPONSE`, `ERR_NETWORK`, `ERR_CANCELED`, `ECONNABORTED`, and `ETIMEDOUT` only when `transitional.clarifyTimeoutError: true`).
- Cancellations: `axios.isCancel(error)` narrows to `CanceledError`, whose `name` is `'CanceledError'`.
- Don't use `error as AxiosError` or `error instanceof AxiosError` as the primary mechanism: the cast is unsound for non-axios throws, and `instanceof` breaks across duplicate axios copies. The flag-based guard handles both.
- For safe logging, `AxiosError.toJSON()` omits the circular `cause` (set non-enumerably by `AxiosError.from`) and honors a `redact: ["authorization"]` key in the request config to mask secrets.

One pattern worth documenting in a client wrapper: make a helper like `isApiError(error): error is AxiosError<ApiErrorBody>` that composes `axios.isAxiosError<ApiErrorBody>` plus `error.response !== undefined`, so callers get a single guard that also guarantees the typed body exists.

