## How fake timers are set up and torn down here

`ModernFakeTimers` is created per test file in the environment constructor ([index.ts:227](/c/Users/91991/.codex/phase16-live/jest/packages/jest-environment-node/src/index.ts:227)), and `teardown()` disposes it once, at the end of the file ([index.ts:248](/c/Users/91991/.codex/phase16-live/jest/packages/jest-environment-node/src/index.ts:248)). Nothing restores real timers between tests. jest-circus's per-test `beforeEach` only handles `resetModules`/`clearMocks`/`resetMocks`/`restoreMocks` ([jestAdapter.ts:50](/c/Users/91991/.codex/phase16-live/jest/packages/jest-circus/src/legacy-code-todo-rewrite/jestAdapter.ts:50)); `clearAllTimers()` runs only inside `resetModules()` ([index.ts:561](/c/Users/91991/.codex/phase16-live/jest/packages/jest-runtime/src/index.ts:561)). The docs state it plainly: `useFakeTimers()` "will affect other tests within the same file" until `useRealTimers()` is called ([JestObjectAPI.md:962](/c/Users/91991/.codex/phase16-live/jest/docs/JestObjectAPI.md:962)).

## Why the test is flaky

The usual cause is state leaking across tests in the same file. If `useFakeTimers()` is called top-level or in one test without a matching `useRealTimers()` in `afterEach`, every later test runs on the frozen clock: pending timers from earlier tests fire when a later test advances time, and any test that awaits a real `setTimeout` hangs until the global timeout. The suite then passes or fails depending on which tests ran before it — the classic "green alone, red in the file" flake.

Two secondary mechanisms, both visible in [modernFakeTimers.ts](/c/Users/91991/.codex/phase16-live/jest/packages/jest-fake-timers/src/modernFakeTimers.ts): the fake clock wraps every sinon API unless listed in `doNotFake` ([line 166](/c/Users/91991/.codex/phase16-live/jest/packages/jest-fake-timers/src/modernFakeTimers.ts:166)), so `Date`, `performance`, `queueMicrotask`, and `nextTick` are frozen too — code paths that wait on `Date.now()` progression hang nondeterministically. And with `advanceTimers` config or `setTimerTickMode('interval')`, fake time advances with real wall time ([line 190](/c/Users/91991/.codex/phase16-live/jest/packages/jest-fake-timers/src/modernFakeTimers.ts:190)), which is inherently machine-speed-dependent. Using sync `advanceTimersByTime` on promise-chained timers is also racy; use `await jest.advanceTimersByTimeAsync(...)`.

## Correct setup/teardown

```js
beforeEach(() => {
  jest.useFakeTimers();
});

afterEach(() => {
  jest.clearAllTimers(); // must come first: it's a no-op once real timers are back (modernFakeTimers.ts:41)
  jest.useRealTimers();
});
```

Put `useFakeTimers()` in `beforeEach` (not top-level, not inside a test), and always pair it with `clearAllTimers()` then `useRealTimers()` in `afterEach`. Never call `runAllTimers()` on self-rescheduling timers — use `runOnlyPendingTimers()` (the default loop limit aborts after 100k timers). To catch the leak, assert `jest.getTimerCount()` is 0 at the end of each timer test.

