## Why scoping works here

`axios.create(config)` in [lib/axios.js](/C:/Users/91991/.codex/phase16-live/axios/lib/axios.js:30) builds a fresh instance via `createInstance(mergeConfig(globalDefaults, instanceConfig))`. Header merging in [mergeConfig.js](/C:/Users/91991/.codex/phase16-live/axios/lib/core/mergeConfig.js:148) deep-merges `AxiosHeaders` into a new plain object, so instance headers never mutate the global defaults. Each `Axios` constructor also creates its own `this.defaults` and its own `interceptors.request` / `interceptors.response` managers ([Axios.js](/C:/Users/91991/.codex/phase16-live/axios/lib/core/Axios.js:31)). Result: any headers or interceptors you add to an instance are invisible to the global `axios` and to every other instance.

## Concrete implementation

```js
import axios from 'axios';

// Static credential, instance-scoped
const api = axios.create({
  baseURL: 'https://api.example.com',
  headers: { 'X-API-Key': 'your-api-key-here' },
});

// Dynamic bearer token, read fresh on every request
api.interceptors.request.use(
  (config) => {
    const token = localStorage.getItem('access_token');
    if (token) config.headers.set('Authorization', `Bearer ${token}`);
    return config;
  },
  null,
  { synchronous: true } // optional; skips the extra Promise microtask
);

// Response interceptor: 401 → refresh once, then retry the original request
api.interceptors.response.use(
  (response) => response,
  async (error) => {
    if (error.response?.status === 401 && !error.config._retry) {
      error.config._retry = true;
      const { data } = await axios.post('/auth/refresh', {
        refreshToken: localStorage.getItem('refresh_token'),
      });
      api.defaults.headers.common['Authorization'] = `Bearer ${data.access_token}`;
      return api(error.config);
    }
    return Promise.reject(error);
  }
);
```

Key details verified against this checkout:

- Request interceptors run after config merge and header flattening, so `config.headers` is already an `AxiosHeaders` instance and `.set()` is safe ([Axios.js](/C:/Users/91991/.codex/phase16-live/axios/lib/core/Axios.js:141)).
- Request interceptors execute LIFO (last registered first), response interceptors FIFO (first registered first) — documented in [interceptors.md](/C:/Users/91991/.codex/phase16-live/axios/docs/pages/advanced/interceptors.md:117) and [AGENTS.md](/C:/Users/91991/.codex/phase16-live/axios/AGENTS.md:61).
- Conditionally skip via `{ runWhen: (config) => ... }`, remove via `eject(id)` or `clear()` ([InterceptorManager.js](/C:/Users/91991/.codex/phase16-live/axios/lib/core/InterceptorManager.js:36)).
- For HTTP Basic, use the `auth: { username, password }` option instead of a manual header; for cookie sessions use `withCredentials: true` — both per [authentication.md](/C:/Users/91991/.codex/phase16-live/axios/docs/pages/advanced/authentication.md:24).

So the complete pattern is: create an instance with `axios.create`, set static creds in its `headers` default, add the dynamic-token request interceptor and the 401-refresh response interceptor on that instance — and the global axios object plus any other instances remain untouched.
